4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-252924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\pi \\end{aligned}],$\nfind $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-252924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries ${\\lambda }_{1},…,{\\lambda }_{n},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(D)=[\\begin{aligned}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡D & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-252924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ea4643011baa08ce614f164a25cebc94985e23735a38af1c21837f9d01af7d1"} {"question" "q-252924" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma