swer.type/math "81"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denotes the $n\\times n$ Jordan block matrix with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $8$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(3))}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $8$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $27$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $27$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(3))^{3} & ={([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{2}(3)\\end{aligned}])}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({J}_{2}(2))^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & ({J}_{2}(3))^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =({J}_{2}(2))^{3}\\,⊕\\,({J}_{2}(3))^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ Since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(3),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =3.$ Since $n=2,$ we need the first derivative of $f$ only, which we have already computed. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3) & {f}^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3} & 3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}27 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(3))^{3}=({J}_{2}(2))^{3}\\,⊕\\,({J}_{2}(3))^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 12 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 27 & 27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 27\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ea4643011baa08ce614f164a25cebc94985e23735a38af1c21837f9d01af7d1"} {"question" "q-253712" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253712" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value