hacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-252980" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-252980" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $M=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{ln}⁡M.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the given matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡M=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $1$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡M & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253508" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ea4643011baa08ce614f164a25cebc94985e23735a38af1c21837f9d01af7d1"} {"question" "q-253508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253508" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $K=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find ${3}^{K/2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={3}^{x/2}$ and the given matrix $K,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{K/2}={3}^{\\frac{1}{2}PJ{P}^{-1}}=P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $4$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{3}^{J/2}=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{K/2} & =P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-9 \\\\ -18 & 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ea4643011baa08ce614f164a25cebc94985e23735a38af1c21837f9d01af7d1"} {"question" "q-253682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9 \\\\ -9 & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 2\\pi  \\\\ 0 & 2\\pi \\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\pi \\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡C$ and the given matrix $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡C=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi \\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡C & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ea4643011baa08ce614f164a25cebc94985e23735a38af1c21837f9d01af7d1"} {"question" "q-253480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]."] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]."] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]."] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]."] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253480" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\,1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $G=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and the given matrix $G,$ we have\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $\\sqrt{3}$ on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+1}$ So, we get\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G & =P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}]\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi +3 & 4\\pi -3 \\\\ 4\\pi  & -4\\pi \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ea4643011baa08ce614f164a25cebc94985e23735a38af1c21837f9d01af7d1"} {"question" "q-253677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2\\pi +3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\pi -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2\\pi +6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi -3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi +3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi -6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\pi +6\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -12 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $K=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K$ and the given matrix $K,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{3}{1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡K & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4ea4643011baa08ce614f164a25cebc94985e23735a38af1c21837f9d01af7d1"} {"question" "q-253441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{4} & -5\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi }{2} & \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -5\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} & -5\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi }{2} & \\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "q-253441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{4} & -3\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & -\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/state.json" "file_sha256" "76111f53c72140f59c241c3f717c2a7d4fe7e3279c7037bd23160a68ed3ffea3"} {"question" "e-16515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/0d46f91f48f583ead1e4f70c42c737b755d79535e5455e0439ee74f648a4a0bf.png)\nfind $\\log D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16515.json" "file_sha256" "f907d1624710663203fbbe0fb03031958d4f23be981aa84e48d4a1e18a1ad958"} {"question" "e-16515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n,$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/fd9855d724ad4cac8d8f5f93aa81455aea3f0848c744b02fbafc2987054dc7d7.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡D & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(100) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{log}⁡(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{log}⁡(10)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16515.json" "file_sha256" "f907d1624710663203fbbe0fb03031958d4f23be981aa84e48d4a1e18a1ad958"} {"question" "e-16516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f\\left(J_4(1) \\right)$ for $f(x)=2^x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16516.json" "file_sha256" "a08da17fa1da86dfe317d0c686f941c56889be7869d2a2a6ab02d658570d44fe"} {"question" "e-16516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda),$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/738039128c2eef5b848f6114c6008188456705206879411c9dab82e919bc18b5.png)\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x) = 2^{x},$ $n=4,$ and $\\lambda=1.$\n\nSince $n=4,$ we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{4}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) & \\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1} & {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {2}^{1} & {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{1} & {2}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & {2}^{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16516.json" "file_sha256" "a08da17fa1da86dfe317d0c686f941c56889be7869d2a2a6ab02d658570d44fe"} {"question" "e-16522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $J_n\\left(\\lambda\\right)$ denote the $n \\times n$ Jordan block matrix with $\\lambda$ on the main diagonal. Then,  find\n$e^{2\\left(J_2(1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_2(3)\\right)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16522.json" "file_sha256" "dc7b4fb3a2c43a3bd6660323315b663cc5805f0cdf923c2063f21886480f2577"} {"question" "e-16522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda),$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/11519fece8adb61a999a33d66d9d61e3052709066702f96b8141a4574a8d86b8.png)\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2({J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(3))} & ={e}^{2([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{2}(3)\\end{aligned}])} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2({J}_{2}(1))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2({J}_{2}(3))}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{2({J}_{2}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{2({J}_{2}(3))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For $J_2(1),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=1.$ Since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\cdot 1} & 2{e}^{2\\cdot 1} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & 2{e}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $J_2(3),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=3.$ Since $n=2,$ we need the first derivative of $f$ only, which we have already computed. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3) & {f}^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\cdot 3} & 2{e}^{2\\cdot 3} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2\\cdot 3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6} & 2{e}^{6} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/cfb6a5fdf144808f6d92f84b76b2fed4e0f56a26437d23a2bb241ed11a2aa754.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16522.json" "file_sha256" "dc7b4fb3a2c43a3bd6660323315b663cc5805f0cdf923c2063f21886480f2577"} {"question" "e-16517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/17b4d68be43e4a4eb895ebbe623b713f1a808153424dd221b7497579d666b00a.png)\n\nGiven that $V = PJP^{-1},$ find $\\sin\\left(\\pi V\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16517.json" "file_sha256" "ad914a416f1389388eab6f475ea5185a73ff0e1c301df606455877ec1c10e91d"} {"question" "e-16517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = S \\cdot f(A) \\cdot S^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x) = \\sin(\\pi x)$ and the given matrix $V,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\pi V) = \\sin\\left(\\pi{PJP^{-1}}\\right) = P \\cdot (\\sin(\\pi J)) \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $-3$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/a8ad3d4a15a5a8e7307fdd6449d8086d6c9c7e656caea877d41f021c7d8584cc.png)\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is $f'(x) = \\pi \\cos \\pi x.$ So, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/9af57a3b00b9c9e64508c119ae77690904bdb8c30b124b8165d4280149361bad.png)\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/assets/ff9333784a9cdd38f510398b48e485764eac400f56c2cc9f2f1a18e5138845d8.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi V) & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi J))\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\pi  \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & -\\pi  \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & -\\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072904/example-16517.json" "file_sha256" "ad914a416f1389388eab6f475ea5185a73ff0e1c301df606455877ec1c10e91d"}] "usage" {"e-16515" [] "q-253311" [] "q-252924" [] "q-252925" [] "e-16517" [] "q-252980" [] "e-16516" [] "q-252926" [] "e-16522" []} "basis" 2665 "retractions" [[17592186157634 :question/problem "Given that\n$D = \\begin{bmatrix}π & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - π & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2π\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $\\cos D$.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\cos D =$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |"] [17592186157639 :question/problem "Let $J_{n}(λ)$ denote an $n \\cdot n$ Jordan block with $λ$ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\sin (J_{3}(π)) =$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |"] [17592186157635 :question/problem "Let $J_{n}(λ)$ denote an $n \\cdot n$ Jordan block with $λ$ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $6$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\sin (J_{4}(\\frac{π}{6})) = \\frac{1}{12}$ |  | $0$ | $6$ | {{field-3}} | $-3$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"computing-the-value-of-a-function-of-a-jordan-matrix\"></a>"] [17592186157636 :question/problem "Let $J_{n}(λ)$ denotes the $n \\cdot n$ Jordan block matrix with $λ$ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\ln (J_{2}(1)⊕J_{2}(e)) =$ |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"function-of-a-jordan-decomposition\"></a>"] [17592186157637 :question/problem "Let $J_{n}(λ)$ denote an $n \\cdot n$ Jordan block with $λ$ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\tan (J_{1}(\\frac{π}{3})⊕J_{2}(\\frac{π}{4})) =$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |"] [17592186156969 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}100 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind $\\log D$."] [17592186156969 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f(λ_{n})\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log D &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\log (100) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\log (1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\log (10)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186156970 :question/problem "Find $f\\left(J_4(1) \\right)$ for $f(x)=2^x$."] [17592186156970 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda)$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & (f^{″}(λ))/(2!) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 1)}(λ))/((n - 1)!) \\\\ 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 2)}(λ))/((n - 2)!) \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2^{x}\n$$\n\n$n=4$, and $\\lambda=1$.\n\nSince $n=4$, we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 2^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 2^{x}\\ln 2 \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 2^{x}(\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= 2^{x}(\\ln 2)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(J_{4}(1)) &= \\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & f^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}f^{″}(1) & \\frac{1}{3!}f^{‴}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & f^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}f^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1) & f^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2^{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2\\ln 2 & (\\ln 2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{3}(\\ln 2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\ln 2 & (\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\ln 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186156972 :question/problem "Let $J_n\\left(\\lambda\\right)$ denote the $n \\times n$ Jordan block matrix with $\\lambda$ on the main diagonal. Then, find\n$e^{2\\left(J_2(1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_2(3)\\right)}$."] [17592186156972 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda)$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & (f^{″}(λ))/(2!) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 1)}(λ))/((n - 1)!) \\\\ 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 2)}(λ))/((n - 2)!) \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2(J_{2}(1)⊕J_{2}(3))} &= e^{2(\\begin{bmatrix}J_{2}(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & J_{2}(3)\\end{bmatrix})} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2(J_{2}(1))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{2(J_{2}(3))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{2(J_{2}(1))}⊕e^{2(J_{2}(3))}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For $J_2(1)$, we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=1$. Since $n=2$, we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n$f(x)|= e^{2x}; f^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x}$\nSo, we get\n$f(J_{2}(1))|= \\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & f^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2 \\cdot 1} & 2e^{2 \\cdot 1} \\\\ 0 & e^{2 \\cdot 1}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2} & 2e^{2} \\\\ 0 & e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- For $J_2(3)$, we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=3$. Since $n=2$, we need the first derivative of $f$ only, which we have already computed. So, we get\n$f(J_{2}(3))|= \\begin{bmatrix}f(3) & f^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2 \\cdot 3} & 2e^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\ 0 & e^{2 \\cdot 3}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{6} & 2e^{6} \\\\ 0 & e^{6}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{2} & 2e^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & e^{6} & 2e^{6} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & e^{6}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186156971 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $V = PJP^{-1}$, find $\\sin\\left(\\pi V\\right)$."] [17592186156971 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S$, where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = S \\cdot f(A) \\cdot S^{-1}\n$$\n\nFor\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sin(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nand the given matrix $V$, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\pi V) = \\sin\\left(\\pi{PJP^{-1}}\\right) = P \\cdot (\\sin(\\pi J)) \\cdot P^{-1}\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $-3$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(-3) & f^{′}(-3) \\\\ 0 & f(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\pi \\cos \\pi x\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(-3) & f^{′}(-3) \\\\ 0 & f(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (πV) &= P \\cdot (\\sin (πJ)) \\cdot P^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & - π \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}π & - π \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}π & - π \\\\ π & - π\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186154651 :answer-field/id] [17592186154651 :answer-field/key] [17592186154651 :answer-field/type] [17592186154652 :answer-field/id] [17592186154652 :answer-field/key] [17592186154652 :answer-field/type] [17592186154653 :answer-field/id] [17592186154653 :answer-field/key] [17592186154653 :answer-field/type] [17592186154654 :answer-field/id] [17592186154654 :answer-field/key] [17592186154654 :answer-field/type] [17592186154655 :answer-field/id] [17592186154655 :answer-field/key] [17592186154655 :answer-field/type] [17592186154656 :answer-field/id] [17592186154656 :answer-field/key] [17592186154656 :answer-field/type] [17592186154657 :answer-field/id] [17592186154657 :answer-field/key] [17592186154657 :answer-field/type] [17592186154658 :answer-field/id] [17592186154658 :answer-field/key] [17592186154658 :answer-field/type] [17592186154659 :answer-field/id] [17592186154659 :answer-field/key] [17592186154659 :answer-field/type] [17592186154660 :answer-field/id] [17592186154660 :answer-field/key] [17592186154660 :answer-field/type] [17592186154661 :answer-field/id] [17592186154661 :answer-field/key] [17592186154661 :answer-field/type] [17592186154662 :answer-field/id] [17592186154662 :answer-field/key] [17592186154662 :answer-field/type] [17592186154663 :answer-field/id] [17592186154663 :answer-field/key] [17592186154663 :answer-field/type] [17592186154664 :answer-field/id] [17592186154664 :answer-field/key] [17592186154664 :answer-field/type] [17592186154665 :answer-field/id] [17592186154665 :answer-field/key] [17592186154665 :answer-field/type] [17592186154666 :answer-field/id] [17592186154666 :answer-field/key] [17592186154666 :answer-field/type] [17592186154667 :answer-field/id] [17592186154667 :answer-field/key] [17592186154667 :answer-field/type] [17592186154668 :answer-field/id] [17592186154668 :answer-field/key] [1